chunks.text:
- Socrates separates arts that aim at the good from mere "experience" that aims only at pleasure. Cookery is a knack that follows routine and never considers the nature of what it serves. Medicine is an art that knows the patient's nature and gives reasons.
- The same split exists for the soul. Whatever aims at pleasure with no thought of good or evil is "flattery." Examples:
  - flute and lyre playing;
  - dithyrambic poetry (Cinesias, Meles);
  - tragedy;
  - much public oratory.
- Poetry stripped of metre is speech to a crowd, so it is a kind of rhetoric. Rhetoric comes in two kinds: flattery and "disgraceful declamation," or the noble kind aimed at improving citizens by saying what is best "whether welcome or unwelcome."
- Callicles cannot name a living orator of the noble kind. Socrates adds that the dead statesmen he offers (Themistocles, Cimon, Miltiades, Pericles) did not distinguish good desires from bad either.

Socrates retells Prodicus's tale of Heracles at the crossroads of youth.

- **Vice** (who says her friends call her Happiness) promises the easiest and pleasantest road: no war or business, every pleasure of food, drink, sight, smell, love and sleep without pain, and the fruits of other people's labour. Her figure is made up to look taller and rosier than nature made it.
- **Virtue** says the gods give nothing lovely and good apart from toil. She lists what each good costs:
  - the favour of the gods requires serving them;
  - the love of friends requires benefiting them;
  - honour from the state requires aiding the state;
  - good harvests require tending the earth and flocks;
  - military power requires learning the arts of war and practising them;
  - bodily strength requires habituating body to mind "with toil and sweat."
- Virtue rebukes Vice's followers. They are satiated before they are hungry, and they need cooks, ice in summer, feather-beds and rockers to produce an appetite or sleep. They have never heard praise or seen one good deed of their own. Vice's followers are "impotent of body" when young and "witless in mind" when old, and they carry the shame of what they have done and the weariness of what is left to do.
- Virtue's followers enjoy food and drink that are sweet because they wait until desire ripens, and their sleep is sweeter than that of those who do not toil. Young and old honour one another, and they are dear to the gods, loved by friends and honoured by their country. At the end they "bloom afresh," their praise resounding for ever.
- Socrates urges Aristippus to lay this to heart and think about the future of his own life.

- Socrates meets Nicomachides, bitter that Athens elected Antisthenes, a wealth-gatherer with no military record, over him. Socrates notes in Antisthenes' favour that he can supply troops, is pertinacious about winning, and chose the best performers whenever he was choragos.
- His principle: "given a man knows what he needs to provide, and has the skill to do so, no matter what the department of things may be--house or city or army--you will find him a good chief and director of the same."
- He lists what a steward and a general share:
  - securing ready obedience;
  - assigning the right tasks to the best qualified;
  - punishing the bad and rewarding the good;
  - winning the goodwill of subordinates;
  - winning allies and supporters;
  - guarding what is in their charge;
  - working painstakingly;
  - facing enemies and getting the better of them.
- On the battlefield the good economist knows "nothing is so advantageous or so lucrative as victory in battle" and nothing so disastrous and expensive as defeat. He seeks out everything conducive to victory, guards against all that leads to defeat, and is cautious about giving battle until preparation is complete, then fights energetically.
- "The difference between the devotion requisite to private affairs and to affairs of state is merely one of quantity." Both deal with human instruments, and success in either goes to those who know how to deal with people.

- Socrates says he has enough. His whole estate, house included, would sell for about five minae. Critobulus's would fetch over a hundred times that.
- Yet Socrates calls Critobulus "singularly poor". His property covers Socrates's wants, but Critobulus's wealth does not cover his obligations.
- He lists those obligations:
  - costly sacrifices to the gods;
  - entertaining foreign guests;
  - feasting fellow citizens to keep their support;
  - state liturgies such as horse-rearing, training choruses and gymnasia;
  - war levies such as trireme pay and war taxes.
- Neglecting any of these, he says, is punished by Athenians as strictly as theft. Critobulus would be only barely well off with three times what he has.
- The worse fault is that Critobulus fancies himself rich, takes no thought to increase his revenue, and amuses himself.
- Socrates's own security is his friends. If he needed anything, they would "deluge my humble living with a flood of plenty" with a trifling contribution. Critobulus's friends are richer than he is but look to him for help.
